From 8d092104a5fc8d9fcf34923d318ed908ea46fa9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:04:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Every rs2check rule can now be shown to go red

A check that cannot fail is worse than no check: it reads as coverage and is not. Two rules in
rs2check.py have shipped INERT - doing nothing, reporting nothing, and the repo printing 0 ERROR
through both:

  * check 11 read its stripped-line variable one statement before it was assigned and reported
    every hit one line late. The tool written to catch off-by-one mistakes shipped with one.
  * check 17 matched a block header with a pattern a header never satisfies, so the whole rule was
    a no-op from the day it was written.

The selftest that existed because of the first one covered eight of the sixteen rules. The other
eight were 1, 2, 5, 12, 13, 15, 16 and 17 - so BOTH rules that shipped inert were in the uncovered
half, and both stayed inert until something unrelated found them.

It covers all sixteen now, and it does it by building a whole miniature content tree - scripts,
configs, a pack directory, a models directory - breaking one thing per rule and running the real
code path end to end. build_tables walks the fixture; every check function runs against it.

TWO THINGS IT ASSERTS THAT THE OLD ONE DID NOT, and both matter:

  * THE LINE, not just the rule. Check 11's inert version DID fire, on every hit, one line late -
    so "did rule 11 appear?" passes on it. ALL_RULES pins file and line for all sixteen.
  * A CLEAN FIXTURE THAT MUST PRODUCE NOTHING. A rule that fires on everything is no more use than
    one that fires on nothing, and three rules here were wrong in that direction on their first
    version: rule 4 flagged 31 files that all compile, rule 13 flagged a load-bearing comma, rule 1
    could not read a block comment. Every idiom in the clean file is one some rule got wrong.

Writing the clean half immediately found two things I had wrong rather than the tool: rule 6 is
right to flag a bare p_finduid(uid) - the repo always tests it - and rule 7 is right to flag
def_namedobj from inv_getobj, because obj does not narrow to namedobj.

AND IT FOUND A THIRD INERT PATH, live: rules 14 and 14b read
`known = T["packs"].get(pack)` and then `if known and sym not in known`, so if synth.pack is
absent or empty, every sound_synth in the repo goes unchecked and the tool still prints 0 ERROR.
That is the same shape as running the tool from the wrong directory, which build_tables already
refuses to do quietly. It now stops on a missing pack and says which rules would have gone blind.

Battery group 65 runs the selftest, so it cannot be forgotten, and checks the missing-pack guard by
RUNNING the tool with synth.pack taken away rather than by reading the source for its message -
because the first version of that check read the text, and a mutation that turned the guard off
without touching the message walked straight past it. Two of the four mutations below survived the
first time for exactly that reason, and both checks are behavioural now.

Four mutations, all red: check 11 made off-by-one again, check 17's header pattern broken again,
the missing-pack guard disabled, and rule 4 made to fire on correct code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Dc7giZ7Qnh4qy1CYPTDrmH
